{
  "id": "missing-number-xor",
  "questionId": "missing-number-xor",
  "slug": "missing-number-xor",
  "title": "Missing Number (XOR Technique)",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Bit manipulation",
  "patterns": [
    "bit manipulation",
    "xor"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "you could sum 0..n and subtract the sum of nums, but that risks overflow on large inputs - is there a bitwise trick that avoids arithmetic sums entirely?",
    "recall XOR cancellation: x XOR x = 0, so if every value from 0 to n appeared exactly twice, XOR-ing them all would give 0",
    "XOR together every number from 0 to n AND every number in nums - every present value gets XORed twice (once from the range, once from nums) and cancels out, leaving only the missing one",
    "start an accumulator at 0, XOR in every index i from 0 to n-1 along with nums[i], then XOR in n itself at the end (or equivalently XOR each index i with nums[i] as you go, then finish with n) - what's left is the missing number"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a running sum formula n*(n+1)/2 instead of the XOR approach the problem is asking you to practice, which can overflow for large n in fixed-width integer languages",
    "forgetting to XOR in n itself (the largest possible value in the range), since nums only has indices 0 to n-1",
    "double-counting or skipping an index when pairing up range values with array values"
  ],
  "content": {
